// Debug-only build settings for the app target, wired in by project.yml's
// `configFiles`. This is the one thing project.yml cannot express: an xcconfig is
// the only place that can `#include?` a file which may not exist.
//
// Why it exists: an ad-hoc signature (`CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = -`) has no stable
// identity, so macOS tells one build from the next by its cdhash. Every rebuild is
// therefore a *new* app to TCC, and an Accessibility, Input Monitoring, or Screen
// Recording grant made for the previous build silently stops applying. Signing with
// a real certificate instead gives the app a designated requirement that survives a
// rebuild, so the grant survives with it.
//
// That certificate is per-machine, so it is never committed. Create
// Config/Local.xcconfig — gitignored, and refused by the commit-time guard
// (scripts/guard/paths.sh) — and set DEVELOPMENT_TEAM and CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY there;
// docs/getting-started.md › "Keeping permission grants across rebuilds" has the
// recipe. With no such file the include below is skipped and the Debug build is
// signed ad hoc exactly as before, so CI and a fresh clone are unaffected.
//
// Release is deliberately absent from `configFiles`: it keeps its settings from
// project.yml alone, so nothing a local file says can reach `just smoke` or the
// release workflow.

CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = -

// Optional, uncommitted, and last, so it can override everything above.
#include? "Local.xcconfig"
